[{:seon.task/name "a-thread-wakes-its-joiners-before-it-leaves-the-live-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-09 by the main-carrier stuck fix (Jolt 869d77f6), left open on purpose rather than patched: a thread's exit (reference-code/jolt/host/chez/lazy-bridge.ss, the exit path near jolt-thread-exit-hook and jolt-thread-end-wake) removes it from live-threads and only after that wakes the threads and fibers joining it, so between the two a deadlock check sees no live thread that may run while a joiner is about to be woken, and can tell that joiner a false deadlock. The patch the fix declined was counting a thread mid-exit as able to run (a special case in the detector). The cause is the order: an ending thread wakes its joiners first and leaves live-threads after, both under the lock the detector reads with, so no check can see the gap. The comment there explains why live-threads is updated before the exit hook (a late parent re-adding an entry); keep that guarantee. Proof, red first: a fiber on the main carrier that joins a thread ending in that window is answered by the join, never told a deadlock (force the window as test/chez/main-carrier-stuck-test.clj forces its)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.host-test/fiber-main-carrier-joins-thread-ending-exit-window}}
 {:seon.task/name "jolt-is-at-upstreams-head"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes. The owner's ruling (docs/decisions.md, 2026-11-10; docs/forks.md#following-upstream). On main: Jolt pinned at `122445b2`, the tip of ``jolt/on-upstream-2026-11-10`` (upstream main `8d3ba3c9` with our commits merged on top), proved whole at its landing. What remains: Jolt's own gates on the pin's line, 13 of 192 red (the table ``target/upstream-work/jolt/pass6.tsv``, on `601c9379`): recordinline, smoke, buildsmoke, largebytesgc, dcerefs, portcheck, deadhost, parkcheck, attributioncheck, certify, gambitgencheck, fiberspoll, gosm; each was red on the pin before too (hostarity and makefilesmoke, red on b72de3d9 on 2026-11-05, are green there). Known causes: fiberspoll's connect tests (macOS refuses a connect to a full backlog at once) and smoke's jolt.socket case are red on upstream as well (task jolt.async/socket-test-runs-green); smoke's malformed-let case is red because our patch `3b693169` gives the JVM's wording on purpose; buildsmoke fails where our release strip deletes a spliced closure's free-variable names (task jolt.backend/build-smoke-is-green-on-the-pin); parkcheck is task jolt.async/the-park-lock-check-is-green-on-the-pin; attributioncheck reads commit messages; the rest are read to no cause. Also: the fork repo's ``jolt/main`` is at `1fad8b83`, under the pin; move it to the pin's line. Not ours to weaken: by the thread law (docs/machine.md) work the old Jolt ran on fibers and the new one runs on OS threads is fixed or configured in the fork, never added to the declared set. Done means: each gate green on the pin, or stated in docs/forks.md#jolt as not ours to hold with why, so a pin's report can say every gate green or name what is not. Proof to write: jolt.host-test/each-red-gate-is-green-or-stated-with-its-reason."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.host-test/each-red-gate-is-green-or-stated-with-its-reason}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-host-method-call-costs-a-dispatch"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-11 while cutting hasch: a method call on a host object costs about 190 ticks on Jolt (`.reset` on a `MessageDigest` alone 187; a SHA-512 of 2,368 bytes through libcrypto is 573), so a loop that calls one host method an element is dominated by dispatch (hasch's digest of 64 uuids: 19.3k of its ticks). A plain protocol method call on a deftype costs 10-13 ticks and allocates nothing (one type at the site), a call of five or more arguments 9 ticks and 80 B plus 16 B an argument (the same on upstream's head). So the 80-208 B a method call seen inside the sorted set, which the waived proof for `seon.db/eid` blames on a deftype method call's site cache (task jolt.backend/a-deftype-method-call-takes-a-site-cache), is more likely a host method call or a five-argument call: not yet measured there. Correct: name which call shape the sorted set's inner loop pays (a profile of 1,000 seeks on the Jolt at upstream's head), correct that waiver's stated cause with it, then cut the shape at its cause (a host site's lookup, or the argument list a wide call builds). Should cost: a host method call within a few times a protocol call."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fibers-own-state-lives-in-its-slice"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes. The second of four Jolt pins that make a fiber's state safe by construction (docs/forks.md#a-fibers-state-is-safe-by-construction; design approved 2026-11-11). What exists: the scheduler saves and restores three thread parameters at every pause (the binding stack, the namespace, the transaction: ``host/chez/fibers.ss``, the dslice); everything else kept per OS thread is shared by the fibers of that thread. Shown red 2026-11-11 on the Jolt branch ``jolt/a-fibers-state-is-its-own`` (``test/chez/fiber-safety.clj``): an eval's byte budget counts the carrier's allocation, so an eval is told it spent its budget for another fiber's allocation (``java/concurrency.ss`` near 2217; Seon reads it in seon.intr); a ``ThreadLocal`` set by one fiber is read by another (``java/host-static-classes.ss`` near 3219); the error-position state is per carrier. Correct: ONE macro, ``define-fiber-state``, is the only way to make state a fiber owns; it adds a slice field the switch saves and restores; ThreadLocal, the byte count and the error position become slice fields; a foreign call's errno is returned with its result inside a region no pause can enter. Gate: a thread parameter or virtual register written by a setter outside the scheduler fails. Cost to measure: ns a slice field adds to a pause. Proof when built: the byte-budget and threadlocal cases of that file green, as a gate of the fork."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-scoped-construct-has-no-entry-hook"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes. The third of the four pins (docs/forks.md#a-fibers-state-is-safe-by-construction). What exists: a pause unwinds and a resume rewinds every ``dynamic-wind`` but a ``finally``, so a before-thunk that sets state runs again on resume. Measured 2026-11-11: a check at every resume that the slice and the winder chain are the objects saved at the pause raised nowhere in Jolt's unit suite and 15 fiber gates; what the compiler and the core macros emit is right; the offenders are host sites: ``jolt-with-out-str`` (fixed, Jolt `ec4c1e44`), the AOT open-compile mark (``host/chez/loader.ss`` near 2033 and 2092: a pause closes the batch and a queued cache write is lost, shown red), and the build-only ``jolt-ns-load-vars-push!``. Correct: four constructors that take no entry hook from their caller (fiber-scoped over the slice, exit-only cleanup as a ``finally``, OS-thread-scoped by parameterize, held mutex), and raw ``dynamic-wind`` nowhere else in the host. Gate: ``dynamic-wind`` and ``jolt-wind*`` appear only inside those four definitions. The resume check stays a finder (5 ns on a 310 ns pause, 1.7%: over the 1% bar for always-on), off in shipped builds. Proof when built: the aot-batch-park case green and the gate."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-shared-table-is-frozen-swapped-or-locked-inside"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes. The fourth of the four pins (docs/forks.md#a-fibers-state-is-safe-by-construction). What exists: the class tables (``class-ctors-tbl``, ``class-statics-tbl``, ``host-class-ctors-tbl``, ``host-class-statics-tbl``, ``host-methods-tbl``, ``host-arity-wrappers``, ``jhost-tag->fqn``: ``java/host-static.ss``) are written by deftype, defrecord and lazy class loads with no lock, and the epoch is bumped before the write. Measured 2026-11-11 with a finder that raises on a write outside the registry's mutex: 99 raises at 6 sites in the unit suite; with the finder off, a racing test lost a record type's constructor in 1 of 6 runs and stalled in 2 of 6 (the stall's cause is not found: find it first). Also unlocked: the jar-entry weak tables, the FFI size table's reads, smaller registries and two once-only latches (the sweep's findings 9 to 12). Correct, per table, one of three forms and no other: filled before a second fiber or thread exists; an immutable value in one box replaced by compare-and-set (reads take no lock); or, for a weak table or a hot write path, a table type whose lock is inside its operations and whose raw table is not reachable. Gate: a process-wide mutable defined any other way fails. Proof when built: the deftype-race case green 100 of 100, and the gate."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-deep-call-with-a-collection-inside-sometimes-counts-96-bytes"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-11 on Chez 4d318683 and Jolt 7135d660: a window of the calling thread's allocated bytes around a 20,000-deep call that asks for a collection at its deepest frame reads 0 B, and in about one window of 200 reads 96 B (1 of 400, 1 of 150, 1 of 10 before the two fixes, when it read 704 B against 608 B). The same count of windows around the deep call alone (600) and around a collection alone (350) never read it, so it needs both: a stack the collection made one multi-shot continuation, and something else landing while it is cut on the way back. Inferred, not shown: the engine's tick handler capturing a continuation on that stack (64 B and a 32 B closure). Reproduction: seon.profile-test/a-new-stack-is-not-the-calls-bytes with its inside window taken 400 times and counted by value. Correct: the source found by reading what allocates 96 B on that path (``S_split_and_resize``, the tick handler in Chez's s/library.ss), then left out of the thread's count where it is made if it is the runtime's own; the test then takes one window, not the least of three."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.profile-test/a-new-stack-is-not-the-calls-bytes}}
